---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { researchIndex, absolute } from '@/lib/agent-knowledge';
const index=researchIndex();
const example=`curl -fsS ${absolute('/agents/research.mjs')} -o research.mjs\nnode research.mjs "persistent agent work"`;
const jsonLd={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'TechArticle',headline:'Research Intelligence Snacks with an agent',url:absolute('/for-agents/'),description:'Public read-only knowledge records, evidence, stable citations and a reference retrieval client.'};
---
<BaseLayout title="For agents — Intelligence Snacks" description="Retrieve shared ideas and source observations from Intelligence Snacks using a public, read-only JSON interface." {jsonLd}>
<main class="page-shell agent-docs"><header><p class="eyebrow">Public research interface · v1</p><h1>Follow the evidence.</h1><p class="intro">Find ideas across the archive, retrieve the original observations, and cite the discussions they came from. No account, API key or visual graph interaction is needed.</p></header>
<section><h2>Start here</h2><p><a href="/ideas/">Read the five shared-idea guides</a>, or download the <a href="/api/knowledge/v1/index.json">research index</a>. It contains {index.counts.ideas} reviewed themes and {index.counts.observations} extracted observations from {index.counts.episodes} episodes.</p><p>This is a static, read-only interface. Search the index locally, then retrieve the matching records. Query parameters do not filter the JSON files. There is no write endpoint, server-side semantic search or MCP server in this release.</p><pre><code>{example}</code></pre><p>The <a href="/agents/research.mjs" download>reference client</a> requires Node.js 18+ (or Bun). It performs simple word matching, retrieves the top five records, and prints source excerpts and citation URLs. Its scores are relevance hints, not confidence in a claim.</p></section>
<section><h2>Endpoints</h2><div class="docs-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>URL</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Index</td><td><a href="/api/knowledge/v1/index.json">/api/knowledge/v1/index.json</a></td><td>Titles, summaries, stable IDs, record URLs, citation URLs, counts and content revision.</td></tr><tr><td>Theme</td><td><a href="/api/knowledge/v1/ideas/graph-memory.json">/api/knowledge/v1/ideas/graph-memory.json</a></td><td>Theme summary, reviewed grouping rationales, source observations, all evidence and related themes.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation</td><td><code>/api/knowledge/v1/observations/&#123;uuid&#125;.json</code></td><td>One observation, source episode, speaker-attributed excerpts, transcript revision and typed incoming/outgoing relationships.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Follow the URLs in the index; theme records use registry slugs and observation records use UUIDs without the <code>knowledge:</code> prefix. Unknown record paths return HTTP 404. Theme records outside the five-page pilot have <code>pageUrl: null</code>; they still provide evidence and a graph URL.</p></section>
<section><h2>How to cite</h2><ol><li>For a source claim, cite the evidence’s <code>sourceUrl</code> and attribute its speaker and episode. A theme grouping does not establish the claim as fact.</li><li>For our synthesis, cite the theme’s <code>pageUrl</code> when present. Cite the original sources as well when discussing their claims.</li><li><code>locator: "transcript-turn"</code> identifies a resolved transcript passage. <code>locator: "episode"</code> means the exact passage was not resolved; its timestamp is null. Do not invent a timestamp or quote a summary as spoken words.</li><li>Shared-theme membership and overlapping observations do not establish agreement, contradiction or causation. Preserve differences between the sources.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>Provenance and record fields</h2><p>Every response has <code>schemaVersion: "1.0"</code> and a deterministic <code>contentRevision</code> hash. The hash changes when the exported public records change; it is not a publication date. Theme <code>review</code> fields identify the agent reviewer, method and review date. Episode <code>publishedAt</code> gives the source publication date when available.</p><p>Observations contain <code>id</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>summary</code>, <code>episode</code>, <code>extractionMethod</code>, <code>transcriptRevisionId</code>, <code>evidence</code> and <code>connections</code>. Evidence contains the original excerpt, attributed speaker, contributor URL where matched, locator, timestamp and source URL. Each connection retains its <code>sourceId</code>, <code>targetId</code>, <code>type</code> and recorded explanation; <code>neighbourId</code>, <code>neighbourTitle</code> and <code>neighbourUrl</code> identify the other end of the relationship, including for incoming links.</p><p>Pilot themes include <code>readingNotes</code> with source observation IDs and <code>guideUpdatedAt</code>; other themes have an empty notes array and null guide date. Themes contain <code>sources</code>, each with its observation, grouping rationale and reviewed evidence IDs. Additional observation excerpts are available context; not every excerpt was separately reviewed for the grouping. <code>relatedIdeas</code> means shared source observations, with an explicit count.</p></section>
<section><h2>Scope and freshness</h2><p>Only accepted public records are exported. Themes were grouped through agent editorial review; observations may be automatically extracted and transcript-validated. These are records of podcast discussions, not independently verified facts or an exhaustive review of every transcript.</p><p>HTML guides are the indexable citation pages; machine exports carry a noindex response header to avoid duplicate search listings, while remaining publicly retrievable. Files refresh when the site is built and deployed. Compare content revisions when caching or combining records. The v1 path preserves this contract; compatible fields may be added. Keep a local index for repeated requests and fetch only the detail records you need.</p><p>Use normal <a href="/sitemap.xml">sitemap</a>, <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt index</a> and <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a> for discovery. Public access does not guarantee inclusion or citation in any search engine or model.</p></section>
</main></BaseLayout>
<style>.agent-docs{max-width:1100px;padding-block:3rem}.agent-docs header{max-width:54rem;margin-bottom:3rem}.agent-docs h1{font:500 clamp(3rem,6vw,5rem)/1 var(--font-display);letter-spacing:var(--tracking-hero);margin:1rem 0 1.5rem}.intro{font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.65}.agent-docs section{margin-block:2.5rem}.agent-docs h2{font:500 1.9rem var(--font-display);letter-spacing:var(--tracking-display)}.agent-docs p,.agent-docs li{line-height:1.75;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.agent-docs li{margin-bottom:.65rem}.agent-docs a{text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px}.agent-docs pre{padding:1.25rem;background:var(--paper-raised);border-radius:8px;overflow:auto;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.8}.agent-docs code{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.85em;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.docs-table{overflow:auto}.docs-table table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6}.docs-table th,.docs-table td{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;padding:1rem .75rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}.docs-table th:first-child,.docs-table td:first-child{padding-left:0}@media(max-width:650px){.agent-docs{padding-block:2rem}.agent-docs pre{padding:.75rem}.docs-table table{min-width:600px}}</style>
